{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"c5ebf15e-9a59-41a6-8e4f-c9b308de50c7","status":"SUCCESS","response":"### Cause\n\n1. **Client-side rendering fetches from `/v1/notes`:** Every `/n/\u003cslug\u003e` route is rewritten by CloudFront to [index.html](file:///tmp/h-wiCJM9/site/index.html) (`HTTP 200`), and [app.js](file:///tmp/h-wiCJM9/site/app.js#L6-L17) fetches note data at runtime via `fetch(\"/v1/notes/\u003cslug\u003e\")` (and `fetch(\"/v1/notes?limit=50\")` on `/`).\n2. **`robots.txt` blocks `/v1/` subresource fetches for Googlebot:** On `2026-09-07`, `Disallow: /v1/` was added to [robots.txt](file:///tmp/h-wiCJM9/site/robots.txt) to prevent crawlers from sending `GET` requests to POST-only API paths ([routes.md](file:///tmp/h-wiCJM9/api/routes.md#L14-L16)). Normal browsers and `curl` do not check `robots.txt`, so pages load normally for users. However, Googlebot's Web Rendering Service enforces `robots.txt` on JavaScript `fetch()` calls and blocks `/v1/notes/\u003cslug\u003e`.\n3. **Blocked `fetch()` triggers the `\"NOT FOUND\"` catch block on an `HTTP 200` page:** When `fetch()` rejects, [app.js](file:///tmp/h-wiCJM9/site/app.js#L18-L20) renders `NOT FOUND / No note lives at this address.` inside the `HTTP 200` shell (matching [url-inspection-drip-line-pressure.txt](file:///tmp/h-wiCJM9/search-console/url-inspection-drip-line-pressure.txt#L7-L9)), which Google Search Console classifies as a **Soft 404**.\n\n### Fix\n\n- Updated [robots.txt](file:///tmp/h-wiCJM9/site/robots.txt#L1-L7) to allow the public note read endpoints (`Allow: /v1/notes`, covering both `/v1/notes?limit=50` and `/v1/notes/{slug}`) while keeping the POST-only reactions path (`Disallow: /v1/notes/*/reactions`) and the remaining `/v1/` routes (`Disallow: /v1/`) blocked.\n- Documented the cause and fix in [ANSWER.md](file:///tmp/h-wiCJM9/ANSWER.md).\n","duration_seconds":335.053917,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":55640,"output_tokens":23314,"thinking_tokens":19227,"cache_read_tokens":325650,"total_tokens":78954}}}
